  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1447",
      "html": "<p>Gagata sexualis Tilak, 1970. Size: 58 mm (2.3 inches) SL. Distinguished by its small size, the presence of 5 dark saddles on the body, an elongate and slender snout, and pronounced sexual dimorphism in the shape of the dorsal fin. IUCN Red List: Least Concern (last assessed 2009).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "beforeYouBuy",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1447",
      "html": "<p>This is a very delicate species, even more so than G. cenia, so it is unlikely to be encountered in the aquarium trade as it fares very poorly upon capture.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1447",
      "html": "<p>Asia: India. Found in the Padma, Jamuna, Brahmaputra, Koel and Ganges drainages, and in West Bengal (Ichamati River). Type locality: North Koel River at Daltonganj, Chotanagpur, southern Bihar, India.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1447",
      "html": "<p>pH 6.0–7.0. Temperature 18–25°C. Requires well oxygenated water.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1447",
      "html": "<p>Mature males have a long filamentous extension to the dorsal fin.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1447",
      "html": "<p>Unreported in the aquarium.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1447",
      "html": "<p>Etymology: the generic name comes from the local name of the fish (keyakatta). The specific epithet comes from the pronounced sexual dimorphism of this species.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
